**Theorem 12.1 (square reduction and directed zero scope).** Under
Assumption 10.1, none of the eight tests (FC.7) is identically zero,
and every zero of $F_{j,\epsilon,\sigma}$ satisfies
$\epsilon u>0$. If a pure test has two distinct zeros, it has the
form $(Au+Bv+C)^2$ on the circle. If a saturated test has four
distinct zeros, it has the form $(E_2(u,v)+L_1(u,v))^2$, where
$E_2$ is a real homogeneous quadratic and $L_1$ is a real linear
form. Multiplicities and the point $(-1,0)$ are included.

Proof. The following homogeneous maximal-zero square fact is the
auxiliary lemma used here. For $d=2$ or $4$, let $T$ be a nonzero
nonnegative real trigonometric polynomial of degree at most $d$.
Under the half-angle map

$$
u=\frac{V_h^2-U_h^2}{U_h^2+V_h^2},\qquad
v=\frac{2U_hV_h}{U_h^2+V_h^2},
\tag{FC.37}
$$

$H=(U_h^2+V_h^2)^dT$ is a nonzero nonnegative real homogeneous
binary form of degree $2d$. The map from $\mathbb{RP}^1$ to the
circle is bijective, including $V_h=0$, which is $(-1,0)$.
At every real projective zero the local polynomial has even
multiplicity, since an odd multiplicity would change its sign.
Thus there are at most $d$ distinct zeros. If there are $d$,
their multiplicities exhaust all $2d$ degrees. Each is exactly
double, and factorization over $\mathbb R$ gives
$H=c_H\prod_{i=1}^d\ell_i(U_h,V_h)^2$ with $c_H>0$ and real
linear $\ell_i$. There is no unaccounted complex factor, lower-degree
exception, or extra point at infinity. Repeated roots consume the
same degree budget and cannot increase the number of distinct zeros.

For $d=2$ the homogeneous quadratic square root is a linear
combination of $V_h^2-U_h^2$, $2U_hV_h$, $U_h^2+V_h^2$.
Dividing by $U_h^2+V_h^2$ gives $Au+Bv+C$. For $d=4$ a
homogeneous quartic square root is a linear combination of the lifts
of $u^2,v^2,uv,u,v$, which form a basis of the five-dimensional
space of real binary quartics. Dividing by $(U_h^2+V_h^2)^2$
gives $E_2+L_1$. The positive constant is absorbed into the square
root. This elementary scalar factor argument is consistent with
Dritschel–Rovnyak, *The Operator Fejér–Riesz Theorem*,
[arXiv:0903.3639v1, §1](https://arxiv.org/html/0903.3639v1),
a survey of the classical factorization. No operator factor rank
or general quadrature node bound is used.

For the source tests, flipping $\epsilon$ changes a pure test by
$2\epsilon\lambda u$ and a saturated test by
$2\epsilon\lambda D_cu$. If one test were identically zero,
the opposite test would change sign, contradicting full-circle
nonnegativity. At a zero with $u\ne0$, the same comparison and
$D_c>0$ imply $\epsilon u>0$. At $u=0$ both report tests
would vanish. Both angular derivatives would be zero by
nonnegativity, but their difference is $2\epsilon\lambda u'$
or $2\epsilon\lambda D_cu'$, and $u'\ne0$ there. This is
impossible. The degree-two and degree-four tests now satisfy the
auxiliary lemma. This also recovers the positive-multiplier parts
of RC Theorem 69.1 and Proposition 70.2 in the present normalization;
no nonidentity assertion at $\lambda=0$ is inferred. $\square$

